course: dictionary
lesson: 970
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Blunt is one of them. Context tells you which meaning the writer means. «A blunt knife is not safer: it needs more force, so when it slips it cuts you.» Here blunt means not sharp.
explain: «Teachers write reports that parents read, so they choose kind, careful words instead of blunt ones.» Here blunt means saying what you think very directly, without trying to be polite. The sentence tells you why: the teachers want to be careful with their words.
explain: To find the right meaning, try the swap check. Put your meaning into the sentence in place of the word. If it still makes sense, you have found the meaning the writer intended.
fact: Blunt means not sharp; it has an edge or point that does not cut well.
fact: Blunt can also mean saying what you think very directly, without trying to be polite.
fact: Blunt can be a verb: to blunt something means to make it less sharp.
fact: Safety scissors for young children have blunt, rounded ends, so they are less likely to hurt you.
fact: A blunt knife can be more dangerous than a sharp one, because you need more force and it may slip.
exercise: «A blunt knife is not safer: it needs more force, so when it slips it cuts you.» What does blunt mean here? | Shiny | Very loud | Kind and polite | *Not sharp
exercise: Mia says, «That drawing is blunt, Sam, you should throw it away!» What does blunt mean in what Mia said? | Made of metal | *Saying what you think very directly, without trying to be polite | Very kind and gentle | Long and thin
exercise: Which word means the opposite of blunt, as in a knife? | Rounded | Blunted | *Sharp | Dull
test: «Years of use had blunted the old pencil.» Here blunted is a verb. What does it mean? | Broke into pieces | *Made less sharp | Made longer and thinner | Painted in bright colours
test: «Cutting hair does not make it grow back thicker; the cut end only looks blunter.» What does blunter mean here? | Curlier | Thicker and stronger | *Less pointed, with a flat end | Darker in colour
exam: «The coach was blunt: "Your running is too slow, and you need to train harder."» What does blunt mean here? | *Saying what he thinks directly, without trying to be polite | Kind and gentle | Not sharp | Very quiet
